{%- comment -%}
  GOD SQUAD — Cart drawer
  Phase 8. Rendered once from layout/theme.liquid, on every template EXCEPT the
  cart page — a drawer showing the same cart as the page behind it is two views
  of one thing that can disagree, and it gave every cart line a duplicate DOM
  id. The layout owns that condition.

  Sources of truth:
  - PHASE-2-DESIGN-SYSTEM.md §17.3 (a drawer is one of the four things allowed
    a shadow, and only with a z-index above --z-sticky, a backdrop and a focus
    trap), §23.6 (the five focus rules, which it says apply to the cart drawer
    verbatim), §12.4 (quantity), §10 (buttons), §21.4 (the scrim's duration).
  - PHASE-1-WEBSITE-AUDIT.md ECOM-02: "There is no cart, cart drawer,
    add-to-cart, quantity control or checkout pathway."

  IT IS A REAL CART FORM FIRST AND A DRAWER SECOND.
  Everything inside is Shopify's documented no-JavaScript cart: a form posting
  to routes.cart_url, one updates[] field per line in cart order, a submit
  named checkout, and item.url_to_remove links. With scripting disabled the
  drawer is simply not openable and the header's cart control is what it has
  always been — a link to the cart page.

  MODAL, WITH inert RATHER THAN A HAND-ROLLED TRAP.
  role="dialog" + aria-modal="true", and on open the script marks the page's
  other top-level regions inert. inert removes them from the tab order, the
  accessibility tree, pointer input and find-in-page in one attribute, which a
  Tab-cycling trap does not do and aria-hidden does worse — aria-hidden leaves
  content focusable while hiding it.

  BOTH FEEDBACK CHANNELS LIVE INSIDE THE DIALOG.
  aria-modal="true" tells assistive technology to treat everything outside this
  element as absent, so the layout's own live region cannot be heard while the
  drawer is open. The drawer carries its own, and its own visible error line —
  a failed quantity change used to be reported only to a screen reader, leaving
  a sighted customer watching a number snap back with no explanation.

  Both sit OUTSIDE [data-cart-drawer-inner], with the heading, because that
  node is replaced wholesale after every cart change: a focused element inside
  it is destroyed, and a live region recreated together with its text announces
  nothing at all.

  WHY IT IS NOT A <dialog>.
  showModal() would give inertness, the top layer, Escape and focus return for
  free. It also promotes the panel out of its stacking context, splits the
  scrim into a ::backdrop pseudo-element that cannot animate as one group with
  the panel, and brings a live hazard into a cart: any stray method="dialog" on
  a control inside it silently closes the dialog and discards the POST.
{%- endcomment -%}

{{ 'section-cart-drawer.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}

<div
  class="cart-drawer surface-dark"
  id="CartDrawer"
  role="dialog"
  aria-modal="true"
  aria-labelledby="CartDrawerTitle"
  data-cart-drawer
  data-auto-open="{{ section.settings.auto_open }}"
  hidden
>
  {%- comment -%}
    The backdrop. Pointer-only: clicking it closes the drawer, and Escape is
    the keyboard equivalent, so nothing is reachable only by pointer.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="cart-drawer__overlay" data-cart-overlay aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <div class="cart-drawer__panel">
    <div class="cart-drawer__header">
      {%- comment -%}
        tabindex="-1" so the script can move focus here on open. This is what
        the dialog pattern asks for when the dialog's body has its own
        structure, as a list of cart lines does: focusing the close button
        announces "Close, button" and tells the customer nothing about what
        just happened, and focusing the first line skips the heading entirely.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <h2 class="cart-drawer__title" id="CartDrawerTitle" tabindex="-1">
        {{ 'cart.title' | t }}
      </h2>
      <button type="button" class="cart-drawer__close" data-cart-close>
        {% render 'icon-close' %}
        <span class="visually-hidden">{{ 'cart.close' | t }}</span>
      </button>
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      The drawer's own announcement region. Empty on render; the script injects
      into it while the drawer is open, and into the layout's region when it is
      closed. See the header note on aria-modal.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="visually-hidden" role="status" aria-live="polite" data-cart-drawer-status></div>

    {%- comment -%}
      The visible failure line. Hidden until a cart request fails, and never
      carrying anything but Shopify's own description or one of the theme's own
      sentences from the locale file.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <p class="cart-drawer__error" data-cart-error role="alert" hidden></p>

    <div class="cart-drawer__inner" data-cart-drawer-inner>
      {%- if cart.item_count > 0 -%}
        <form
          action="{{ routes.cart_url }}"
          method="post"
          id="CartDrawerForm"
          class="cart-drawer__form"
          data-cart-form
        >
          <div class="cart-drawer__scroller" data-cart-scroller>
            <ul class="cart-drawer__lines" role="list">
              {%- for item in cart.items -%}
                {% render 'cart-line-item',
                  item: item,
                  index: forloop.index,
                  form_id: 'CartDrawerForm',
                  scope: 'CartDrawer',
                  compact: true
                %}
              {%- endfor -%}
            </ul>

            {%- comment -%}
              Phase 14. Shopify's own cart note, off unless the merchant turns
              it on.

              IT IS IN THE SCROLLER, NOT THE FOOTER.
              The footer is chrome: the totals and the two actions, the things
              that must stay on screen whatever the cart holds. The note is
              content, and putting it in the footer made the footer grow with
              it. Measured at 812x375 — a phone in landscape — with a note
              written: the footer became 451px tall in a 375px viewport and the
              line scroller collapsed to 0px against 474px of cart lines. The
              customer could read their note and could reach Checkout, and
              could not see or change a single thing they were buying.

              That is the same failure the landscape block in
              section-cart-drawer.css was written to fix, arriving by a
              different route, and re-proportioning the chrome would not have
              fixed it because the note has no fixed size to re-proportion.

              Inside the form still, so a native submit posts it.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- if section.settings.show_note -%}
              {% render 'cart-note', id: 'CartDrawerNote' %}
            {%- endif -%}
          </div>

          <div class="cart-drawer__footer">
            {% render 'cart-totals' %}

            {%- comment -%}
              The no-JavaScript update control, and it is FIRST in the form's
              submit order on purpose. A browser submits a form with the first
              submit button when Enter is pressed in a field, so if the
              checkout button came first, pressing Enter after typing a
              quantity would take the customer to checkout instead of applying
              the change they had just typed.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <button type="submit" name="update" class="cart-drawer__update visually-hidden visually-hidden--until-focus">
              {{ 'cart.update' | t }}
            </button>

            <div class="cart-drawer__actions">
              {%- comment -%}
                A submit named checkout, which is Shopify's own cart form
                control: it applies any pending quantity edits and then goes to
                checkout, in one action. A plain link to /checkout would
                silently discard an edit the customer had just made.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              <button
                type="submit"
                name="checkout"
                class="button button--primary button--full"
                data-cart-checkout
              >
                {{ 'cart.checkout' | t }}
              </button>

              {%- if section.settings.show_view_cart -%}
                <a class="button button--secondary button--full" href="{{ routes.cart_url }}">
                  {{ 'cart.view_cart' | t }}
                </a>
              {%- endif -%}
            </div>

            {%- comment -%}
              Continue shopping. A BUTTON that closes the drawer, not a link
              somewhere — the customer is already on the page they were
              shopping, and the drawer is a layer over it, so returning them to
              it is the whole action. A link would navigate them away from the
              thing they were looking at in order to let them carry on looking
              at it.

              The close X above does the same job; this is the same action
              named in words, for a customer who does not read an X as "back to
              what I was doing". It reuses [data-cart-close], so it is the
              drawer's one close path rather than a second one.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <button type="button" class="cart-drawer__continue" data-cart-close>
              {{ 'cart.continue_shopping' | t }}
            </button>
          </div>
        </form>
      {%- else -%}
        {% render 'cart-empty-state',
          url: section.settings.empty_link,
          label: section.settings.empty_link_label
        %}
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Cart drawer",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "auto_open",
      "label": "Open the drawer after adding to the cart",
      "default": true,
      "info": "When this is off, adding still updates the cart count and announces the addition; the customer is simply not interrupted."
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "show_view_cart",
      "label": "Show the View cart link",
      "default": true
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "show_note",
      "label": "Let customers add an order note",
      "default": false,
      "info": "Adds Shopify's own cart note. Turn this on only if you act on what customers write there — a field nobody reads is worse than no field. The cart page has its own copy of this setting, under Cart."
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Empty cart"
    },
    {
      "type": "url",
      "id": "empty_link",
      "label": "Where the empty-cart button goes",
      "info": "Leave empty to use the home page. The cart page has its own copy of this setting, under Cart."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "empty_link_label",
      "label": "Empty-cart button label",
      "default": "Shop the collection",
      "info": "The cart page has its own copy of this setting, under Cart."
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
